      "[JavaScript] Easy to understand - 2 solutions poppinlp 1703 1715 Apr 05, 2021 JavaScript SOLUTION 1 \n We could solve it easily via spliting. \n const   truncateSentence   =   ( s ,  k )   =>  s . split ( ' ' ,  k ) . join ( ' ' ) ; \n SOLUTION 2 \n Let's see if we could have a better performance. \nSince there's no leading or trailing space, and words that are separated by only a single space, so if we want only the first  k  words, we could just count  k  spaces. \nAnd, don't forget to return whole  s  if there are not enough words. \n const   truncateSentence   =   ( s ,  k )   =>   { \n    let  spaceCount  =   0 ; \n    for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  s . length ;   ++ i )   { \n      if   ( s [ i ]   ===   ' '   &&   ++ spaceCount  ===  k )   return  s . slice ( 0 ,  i ) ; \n    } \n    return  s ; \n } ; Comments (3) vidamaleki Aug 09, 2021 I used your first method but I didn't use slice... \nvar truncateSentence = function(s, k) { \nreturn s.split(' ', k).join(' '); \n}; \n this works fine too 5 Show 5 Replies Reply jongourley99 Jun 19, 2021 You can decrement k and check for when it's 0 instead of using spaceCount. 1 Show 2 Replies Reply scraych Apr 27, 2021 Good. Nothing to add here. 0 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "One line code. Up vote only if you understand. mithunmohandas2 24 326 Oct 30, 2023 JavaScript Approach \n\n Split the string at place where space comes. Slice the resultant from index 0 to k (Since we need exactly k words). then join them back with space in between. \n Code \n  \n var  truncateSentence  =   function   ( s ,  k )   { \n   return  s . split ( ' ' ) . slice ( 0 , k ) . join ( \" \" )    } Comments (2) Veshal-Raj Oct 31, 2023 Great solution, Easy to grab. 2 Reply Muhammedswadiq-PV Nov 07, 2023 Great solution 0 Reply",
      "Solution using the length property sffafsdgsdfg 6 458 Feb 08, 2023 JavaScript const  truncateSentence  =   ( s ,  k )   =>   { \n      const   array   =  s . split ( ' ' ) ; \n     \n      array . length  =  k ; \n \n      return   array . join ( ' ' ) ; \n } ; Comments (1) noname09011 Feb 09, 2023 I love you 2 1 Reply",
      "1 Line Solution sajjadbhuiyan 28 636 Mar 02, 2023 JavaScript Code \n  \n var  truncateSentence  =   function ( s ,  k )   { \n      return  s . split ( \" \" ) . splice ( 0 ,  k ) . join ( \" \" ) ; \n } ; Comments (3) user8307YD Mar 02, 2023 thanks 1 Reply iamgamer2022 Mar 02, 2023 It's really work and beat 90% 1 Reply ashrafpallat123 Oct 30, 2023 wow that's crazy 0 Reply",
