      "[JavaScript] Elegant solution using a set and reduce (with explanation) mrhyde 224 2929 Jan 12, 2021 JavaScript Ordered Set It's a pretty straight forward solution. All we need to do is: \n \n create a set containing all allowed characters, \n iterate over all words using reduce accumulating the number of  good  words \n split each word into the array of letters \n check if  every  letter is present in a set of allowed words \n if so - increment the accumulator \n return accumulated value \n \n const   countConsistentStrings   =   ( allowed ,  words )   =>   { \n    let  set  =   new   Set ( allowed ) \n    return  words . reduce ( ( a ,  w )   =>   { \n      return  w . split ( '' ) . every ( l   =>  set . has ( l ) )   ?   ++ a  :  a\n    } ,   0 ) \n } Comments (7) uniqueorn Feb 23, 2022 6 Reply rbardini Dec 30, 2021 3 Reply ar2zee Feb 02, 2021 Why  ++a  and not  a++  I see that it's not working but I don't understand why ? 1 Show 2 Replies Reply yash2709 May 05, 2021 why making set of allowed? as it going to be always distinct? 0 Show 2 Replies Reply chanocuaalejandro Jun 22, 2022 What would be the time complexity? 0 Reply Siva56 Nov 16, 2021 brilliant 0 Reply dhtmlkitchen May 25, 2024 Read more -1 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "All Solutions Short JavaScript Solution Using Regex sronin 348 1129 May 14, 2022 JavaScript Found these solution helpful? Consider showing support by upvoting this post. \nHave a question? Kindly leave a comment below. \nThank you and happy hacking! \n var   countConsistentStrings   =   function   ( allowed ,  words )   { \n      const  re  =   new   RegExp ( ` ^[ ${ allowed } ]+$ ` ) ; \n      let  count  =   0 \n \n     words . forEach ( word   =>   { \n          if   ( word . match ( re ) )  count ++ \n      } ) \n \n      return  count ; \n } ; Comments (0) No comments yet. 1 2 3 4 5 6 class   Solution  { public:      int   countConsistentStrings ( string   allowed ,  vector < string > &   words ) {              } }; Saved Ln 1, Col 1 You need to  Login / Sign up  to run or submit allowed = \"ab\" words = [\"ad\",\"bd\",\"aaab\",\"baa\",\"badab\"] 1 \"ab\" Source word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1",
      "[JS] Straightforward one-liner ekirillov 9 755 Feb 28, 2021 JavaScript const   countConsistentStrings   =   ( allowed ,  words )   =>  \n     words . filter ( \n          word   =>   [ ... word ] . every ( letter   =>  allowed . includes ( letter ) ) \n      ) . length Comments (1) WalkerCai Sep 01, 2021 Hello, would you explain the code,please？ 1 Show 1 Replies Reply",
      "All Solutions faster than 100.00% Intuitive Javascript Solution qkrrbtjd90 310 1393 Dec 30, 2020 JavaScript Solution 1 - save words into an array, then get length of the array; \n const  countConsistentStrings  =   ( allowed ,  words )   =>   { \n    let  result  =   [ ] ; \n \n    for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  words . length ;  i ++ )   { \n      const  word  =  words [ i ] \n \n      for   ( let  j  =   0 ;  j  <  word . length ;  j ++ )   { \n        const   char   =  word [ j ] ; \n \n        if   ( ! allowed . includes ( char ) )   break ; \n        else   if   ( j  == =  word . length  -   1 )  result . push ( word ) \n      } \n    } \n \n    return  result . length ; \n } ; \n Solution 2 - count number of allowed words \n const   countConsistentStrings   =   ( allowed ,  words )   =>   { \n \t let  count  =   0 ; \n \n \twords . forEach ( word   =>   { \n \t\t for   ( let  j  =   0 ;  j  <  word . length ;  j ++ )   { \n \t\t\t const  char  =  word [ j ] ; \n \n \t\t\t if   ( ! allowed . includes ( char ) )   break ; \n \t\t\t else   if   ( j  ===  word . length   -   1 )  count ++ ; \n \t\t } \n \t } ) ; \n \n \t return  count ; \n } ; Comments (0) No comments yet. 1 2 3 4 5 6 class   Solution  { public:      int   countConsistentStrings ( string   allowed ,  vector < string > &   words ) {              } }; Saved Ln 1, Col 1 You need to  Login / Sign up  to run or submit allowed = \"ab\" words = [\"ad\",\"bd\",\"aaab\",\"baa\",\"badab\"] 1 \"ab\" Source word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1",
      "2 Approach (solutions) | Fully Explained 💯 | Simple and Efficient Code 💯🔥 RockAdil 24 334 Aug 24, 2023 JavaScript Array Hash Table String 1+ 1. (Set and Reduce) Approach \n Intuition \n We're counting the number of words in an array that consist only of characters from a given set of allowed characters. \n Approach \n \n Convert the allowed characters into a set for quick lookup. \n For each word in the words array, split the word into individual characters. \n Check if every character in the word exists in the allowed set using the every function. \n If all characters are allowed, increment the count. \n Return the final count. \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n * m) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(m) \n \n\n Code \n var   countConsistentStrings   =   function   ( allowed ,  words )   { \n   allowed  =   new   Set ( allowed ) ; \n \n    return  words . reduce ( ( count ,  word )   =>   { \n      return  word . split ( '' ) . every ( l   =>  allowed . has ( l ) )   ?   ++ count  :  count ; \n    } ,   0 ) ; \n } ; \n \n \n 2. (Brute Force) Approach \n Intuition \n We're counting the number of words in an array that only contain characters from a given set of allowed characters. \n Approach \n \n Initialize a count variable to keep track of the consistent words. \n For each word in the words array:\n \n Initialize a consistent flag as true. \n For each letter in the word:\n \n Check if the letter is not included in the allowed        characters using the includes function. \n If not allowed, set consistent to false and break out of the loop. \n \n \n If consistent is still true, increment the count. \n \n \n Return the final count. \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity:  O(n * m) \n \n\n \n Space complexity:  O(1) \n \n\n Code \n var   countConsistentStrings   =   function   ( allowed ,  words )   { \n    let  count  =   0 ; \n \n    for   ( let  word  of  words )   { \n      let  consistent  =   true ; \n      for   ( let  letter  of  word )   { \n        if   ( ! allowed . includes ( letter ) )   { \n         consistent  =   false ; \n          break ; \n        } \n      } \n     consistent  ?  count ++   :   '' ; \n    } \n \n    return  count ; \n } ; \n \n \n Given the analysis, while this approach works, it has a higher time complexity compared to the first approach using  Set and reduce . Therefore, if performance is a concern, the first approach is generally preferred. 👍 \n If any doubts comment it.. ✨ \n Happy Coding :) \n <img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/users/images/770195a1-d985-4304-ac77-72768cc8d94b_1692887191.8325512.jpeg\" alt=\"upvote.jpeg\"> Comments (2) adilahamed189 Aug 24, 2023 Thanks bro!! 1 Reply Rickey07 Oct 18, 2023 First approach is good bro but generally it uses JS in built methods which interviewer or anyother person would not let us use 0 Show 1 Replies Reply"
