      "JavaScript less than 86.85% and faster than 91.23% seredulichka 68 1882 Aug 25, 2022 JavaScript const   largestAltitude   =   gain   =>   {     \n      let  previous  =   0 ; \n      let  biggest  =   0 ; \n     \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  gain . length ;  i  +=   1 )   { \n         previous  +=  gain [ i ] ; \n          if   ( previous  >  biggest )  biggest  =  previous ; \n      } \n     \n      return  biggest ; \n } ; Comments (1) giogokul13 Feb 12, 2024 Good one 0 Reply",
      "Simple and Easy to Understand with Explanation qkrrbtjd90 310 1196 Mar 24, 2021 JavaScript Video Explanation \n \n There are two ways to solve this: \n \n using arrays to store altitudes then finding max altitude in that array \n store max and current altitude to a variable, then compare current altitude to max altitude. If current altitude is greater simply overwrite max altitude \n \n  \n \n Runtime: 80 ms, faster than 61.27% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \nMemory Usage: 38.7 MB, less than 39.62% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \n \n const   largestAltitude1   =   gain   =>   { \n \t const  altitudes  =   [ 0 ] ; \n \n \t for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  gain . length ;  i ++ )   { \n \t\taltitudes . push ( altitudes [ i ]   +  gain [ i ] ) ; \n \t } \n \n \t return   Math . max ( ... altitudes ) ; \n } ; \n \n Runtime: 80 ms, faster than 61.27% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \nMemory Usage: 38.7 MB, less than 39.62% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \n const   largestAltitude2   =   gain   =>   { \n \t return   Math . max ( \n \t\t ... gain . reduce ( ( acc ,  cv ,  i )   =>   ( acc . push ( cv  +  acc [ i ] ) ,  acc ) ,   [ 0 ] ) \n \t ) ; \n } ; \n  \n \n Runtime: 76 ms, faster than 80.64% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \nMemory Usage: 38.4 MB, less than 70.57% of JavaScript online submissions for Find the Highest Altitude. \n const   largestAltitude3   =   gain   =>   { \n \t let   [ max ,  currentAltitude ]   =   [ 0 ,   0 ] ; \n \n \t for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  gain . length ;  i ++ )   { \n \t\tcurrentAltitude  =  gain [ i ]   +  currentAltitude ; \n \t\t if   ( currentAltitude  >  max )  max  =  currentAltitude ; \n \t } \n \n \t return  max ; \n } ; Comments (2) mrhyde Apr 08, 2021 Read more 1 Show 1 Replies Reply maddhruv Jun 17, 2021 The reduce code is actually very neat and short solution to this. \nIn general I would recommend not using Math.max when we can compare with an iterator in loops cycles 0 Reply",
      "All Solutions 🚴‍♂️ Find the Highest Altitude | LeetCode Solutions in Python 💻 vanAmsen 11282 187 Jun 19, 2023 Python3 C# JavaScript Go 1+ Intuition \n My first thought to solve this problem is to use an incremental approach. Given that we start at altitude 0, we can keep adding the 'gain' at each step to get the new altitude and track the maximum altitude reached at any point. This approach makes sense as the altitude at a given point is simply the sum of all the gains till that point. \n \n Approach \n We start by initializing two variables, 'max_altitude' and 'current_altitude', both to 0. Then, we loop through the 'gain' list, adding each value to 'current_altitude'. After each addition, we compare 'current_altitude' with 'max_altitude'. If 'current_altitude' is greater, we update 'max_altitude' to the new value. The logic behind this is simple - we are climbing up (or down) a mountain, and we keep track of the highest point we've reached so far. Once we've gone through the entire list, we return 'max_altitude' as the highest altitude reached. \n Complexity \n \n \n Time complexity: \nThe time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the length of the 'gain' list. This is because we are traversing the 'gain' list once. \n \n \n Space complexity: \nThe space complexity of the solution is O(1). This is because we are using a constant amount of space to store the current and maximum altitudes, regardless of the input size. \n \n \n Code \n Python JavaScript Rust Go C# var   largestAltitude   =   function ( gain )   { \n      let  maxAltitude  =   0 ; \n      let  currentAltitude  =   0 ; \n     \n      for ( let  g  of  gain )   { \n         currentAltitude  +=  g ; \n          if ( currentAltitude  >  maxAltitude ) \n             maxAltitude  =  currentAltitude ; \n      } \n      return  maxAltitude ; \n } ; Comments (0) No comments yet. 1 2 3 4 5 6 class   Solution  { public:      int   largestAltitude ( vector < int > &   gain ) {              } }; Saved Ln 1, Col 1 You need to  Login / Sign up  to run or submit gain = [-5,1,5,0,-7] 1 [-5,1,5,0,-7] Source word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1",
      "All Solutions beats 97%+ || beginner friendly solution rakshiit 5 1473 Apr 05, 2023 JavaScript Code \n  \n var   largestAltitude   =   function ( gain )   { \n      const  temp  =   [ 0 ] ; \n      let  alt  =   0 ; \n      for   ( let  i  =   0 ;  i  <  gain . length ;  i ++ )   { \n         alt  +=  gain [ i ] ; \n         temp . push ( alt ) ; \n      }   return   Math . max ( ... temp ) ; \n } ; Comments (0) No comments yet. 1 2 3 4 5 6 class   Solution  { public:      int   largestAltitude ( vector < int > &   gain ) {              } }; Saved Ln 1, Col 1 You need to  Login / Sign up  to run or submit gain = [-5,1,5,0,-7] 1 [-5,1,5,0,-7] Source word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word word mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1 mmMwWLliI0fiflO&1",
