      "【Video】How we think about a solution - Python, JavaScript, Java, C++ niits 10969 1397 Oct 01, 2023 C++ Java Python3 JavaScript Welcome to my post! This post starts with \"How we think about a solution\". In other words, that is my thought process to solve the question. This post explains how I get to my solution instead of just posting solution codes or out of blue algorithms. I hope it is helpful for someone. \n Intuition \n Split input string and then reverse each word. \n \n Solution Video \n \n ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n ■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 2,560 \nMy initial goal is 10,000 \nThank you for your support! \n \n Approach \n How we think about a solution \n To be honest, Intuition is almost answer. \n First of all, \n \n ⭐️ Points \n As far as I know, in many language such as Python, Java, strings have the property of immutability which means they cannot be mutated or changed. You can assign strings to variables, and reassign new strings to the same variable, but individual characters within a string cannot be reassigned. \n \n So, we need to convert input string to something then manipulate it. \n My idea is to convert the string to array of each word then reserve each word and concatenate all words. \n Input :   \"Let's take LeetCode contest\" \n At first, We use  split  to create array of each word. \n [ \"Let's\" ,   'take' ,   'LeetCode' ,   'contest' ] \n Then reserve each word \n [ \"s'teL\" ,   'ekat' ,   'edoCteeL' ,   'tsetnoc' ] \n At last, concatenate all the reserved words with  space . \n Output :   \"s'teL ekat edoCteeL tsetnoc\" \n This is easy! Let's see a real algorithm! \n Algorithm Overview: \n \n Split the input string into words. \n Reverse each word. \n Join the reversed words to form the final result. \n \n Detailed Explanation: \n \n \n Splitting the input string into words: \n \n The input string  s  is split into words using the  split()  method, which separates the string based on whitespace, creating a list of words. \n words = s.split() \n \n \n \n Reversing each word: \n \n For each word in the list of words, a list comprehension is used to reverse the characters of each word using slicing ( word[::-1] ). \n reversed_words = [word[::-1] for word in words] \n \n \n \n Joining the reversed words to form the final result: \n \n The reversed words are joined using the  join()  method, with a space as the separator, to create a single string with reversed words separated by spaces. \n The resulting string is returned as the output. \n return ' '.join(reversed_words) \n \n \n \n \n Complexity \n \n Time complexity: O(n) \n \n\n \n Space complexity: O(n) \n \n\n Python JavaScript Java C++  \n var   reverseWords   =   function ( s )   { \n      // Split the input string into words and reverse each word \n      const  words  =  s . split ( ' ' ) ; \n      const  reversedWords  =  words . map ( word   =>  word . split ( '' ) . reverse ( ) . join ( '' ) ) ; \n     \n      // Join the reversed words to form the final result \n      return  reversedWords . join ( ' ' ) ;     \n } ; \n Thank you for reading my post. \n⭐️ Please upvote it and don't forget to subscribe to my channel! \n My previous post for daily coding challenge \n https://leetcode.com/problems/132-pattern/solutions/4107495/video-how-we-think-about-a-solution-stack-python-javascript-java-c/ Comments (5) niits Oct 01, 2023 ⭐️⭐️ Don't forget to subscribe to my channel! ⭐️⭐️ \n■ Subscribe URL \n http://www.youtube.com/channel/UC9RMNwYTL3SXCP6ShLWVFww?sub_confirmation=1 \n Subscribers: 2,560 \nMy initial goal is 10,000 \nThank you for your support! 6 Reply Pat340 Oct 01, 2023 Easy to understand as always. Thanks! 2 Show 1 Replies Reply Mrgupta Oct 01, 2023 CHECK OUT THIS SOLUTION WHICH IS REALLY HELPFUL AND SIMPLE TO UNDERSTAND :  https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-words-in-a-string-iii/discuss/4116145/C%2B%2B-SOLUTION-or-EASY-AND-FRIENDLY-or-STACK-or-SIMPLE 0 Reply agr__rachit Oct 01, 2023 you can join this group for coding related discussion:- \n https://chat.whatsapp.com/Ly8m0CLglOWFWtzp1XIWqC 0 Reply SergeyZhirkov Oct 01, 2023 Please Upvote my solution, if you find it helpful :) \nEasy 1 line solution (Java Stream) \n https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-words-in-a-string-iii/solutions/4111501/easy-java-1-line-solution-java-stream/ 0 Reply",
      "JavaScript solution using the two pointers technique tamara-snyder 54 2413 Oct 26, 2021 JavaScript Two Pointers This solution uses the two pointers technique to reverse the characters in each word of the string. It's middle of the road in time and space complexity and there are definitely better solutions out there, but this one's for the folks looking for a simple explanation. \n The highest level of pseudocode for this problem would look like this: \n \n Split the string into an array of words \n Reverse the characters in each individual word \n Return the recombined string \n \n Here's how this code might look: \n var   reverseWords   =   function ( s )   { \n \t // 1. Split the string into an array of words \n     s  =  s . split ( ' ' ) \n \t\n \t // 2. Reverse the characters in each individual word \n      for   ( let  word  =   0 ;  word  <  s . length ;  word ++ )   { \n         s [ word ]   =   reverse ( s [ word ] ) \n      }  \n \t\n \t // 3. Return the recombined string \n      return  s . join ( ' ' ) \n } ; \n But how do we reverse the characters in a word without using the built in reverse method? This is where the two pointers technique comes in! We'll build a separate helper function,   reverse(word) , to implement this. \n The pseudocode for  reverse(word)  would look like this: \n \n Split the word into an array of characters, e.g.  ['h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'] \n Initialize our two pointers:  left  and  right .  left = 0  and  right = word.length - 1  . So  word[left] === 'h'  and  word[right] === 'o' . \n Swap  left  and  right . We need a new variable  temp  for this. Now  word[left] === 'o'  and  word[right] === 'h' . \n Move  left  one character to the right and  right  one character to the left ( left++, right--) . This will keep us moving towards the middle of the array. Now  word[left] === 'e'  and  word[right] === 'l' . \n Repeat steps 1-4 while  left < right . Once  left === right , we've reached the middle of the array, and we know all necessary characters have been swapped. \n Join the array and return it as a string \n \n function   reverse ( word )   { \n \t // 1. Split the word into an array of characters \n     word  =  word . split ( '' ) \n \t\n \t // 2. Initialize our two pointers, left and right \n      for   ( let  left  =   0 ,  right  =  word . length   -   1 ;  left  <  right ;  left ++ ,  right -- )   { \n \t\t // 3. Swap the left and right characters with the help of a temp variable \n          const  temp  =  word [ left ] \n         word [ left ]   =  word [ right ] \n         word [ right ]   =  temp\n \t\t\n \t\t // 4. Increment left and decrement right to move towards the middle of the array \n \t\t // 5. Repeat the loop while left < right \n \t\t // (The above steps are implemented in the definition of the for loop) \n      } \n \n \t // 6. Join the array and return it as a string \n      return  word . join ( '' ) \n } \n Combine these functions and you've got yourself a solution! Thanks for reading and hope this helps somebody! Comments (6) tomasvzn Mar 16, 2022 I actually didn't know you could do this: \n for (let left = 0, right = word.length - 1; left < right; left++, right--) { \n thanks! 4 Reply kwedgemusic May 18, 2022 Nice One ! 2 Reply arub0419 Jun 25, 2022 Thanks for the approach, it really helped me..  @tamara-snyder 1 Reply mochi_warrior Jan 16, 2022 Thanks for sharing the explanation! It is very helpful. :) 1 Show 1 Replies Reply Kazakov-al Feb 08, 2023 Господи, это великолепно. 0 Reply brpradeepprabhu90 Jul 28, 2022 you dont need to split in the reverse function \n word = word.split('') 0 Reply"
